{"review_id":"00001788597053154009-0003279353-000002-cycle-review-1","reviewed_manifest_hash":"sha256:6949dd65fbfa047f0da3f02cb20eeaa3be15fbe163b26507d214bfdde48ac041","findings":[{"finding_id":"F1-bare-listing-drops-json","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Bare `scope-decisions` no longer emits machine-readable JSON in non-interactive use","claim":"The previous no-argument path printed one `serde_json::to_string(item)` line per pending decision unconditionally, and only added interactive prompts when stdin/stderr were terminals. The new path delegates every no-argument invocation to `list_or_decide_interactively`, which prints only the human `describe_pending_decision` line regardless of `attended`. Acceptance criterion 2 scopes the numbered picker to \"on a terminal\"; the change also replaces the non-terminal output, so any script, daemon, or automation that parsed the JSON listing (the only machine-readable enumeration of pending scope decisions on this command) now receives prose it cannot parse, and the finding hash it needs for the flag form is deliberately absent from that prose. The new integration test `bare_scope_decisions_cli_lists_without_a_hash` runs the binary with a non-terminal stdout and asserts the numbered prose, pinning the removal rather than the terminal-only behavior the criterion asks for.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/cli/scope_decisions.rs","range":{"start":28,"end":43}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/cli/scope_decisions.rs","range":{"start":67,"end":173}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:6819f07e7d61fd0bea8df7a32b6ad1c35bf71b6dc98c85c989f0e7d95b48460c","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":77449,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788596824257818-0003279353-000000/PRD-83","revision":"sha256:bb6c3b4d15d3f6b3aaf9e7769d13ddd95f44c636916f1a88f765e85dfa5f687d","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-6819f07e7d61fd0bea8df7a32b6ad1c35bf71b6dc98c85c989f0e7d95b48460c-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Branch the listing on `attended`: keep the numbered, hash-free prose for the terminal path the PRD specifies, and keep emitting the prior one-JSON-object-per-line listing when stdout is not a terminal (or expose it behind an explicit `--json` flag and keep the old default for non-tty). Add a regression that runs the bare command with a non-terminal stdout and asserts the machine-readable form still parses.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"F2-prd-selection-ambiguous","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Selecting by PRD identifier silently decides only the first of several findings for that PRD","claim":"`list_or_decide_interactively` resolves a non-ordinal selection with `pending.iter().find(|item| item.prd_id.eq_ignore_ascii_case(selection))` and then decides exactly that one finding before returning. A single paused attempt routinely carries more than one pending finding — `drive.rs` passes a `pending_hashes` slice and loops over it — so when two or more rows share a `prd_id`, typing that PRD id approves or rejects an arbitrary (first-listed) finding and returns success, leaving the remaining findings pending with no indication that the PRD was only partially decided. The operator reasonably reads \"decide by PRD identifier\" as deciding that PRD; the attempt then stays blocked for a reason the surface just told them they resolved. The same one-decision-per-invocation limit hits the drive's attended pause, which calls this function once and prints nothing further.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/cli/scope_decisions.rs","range":{"start":67,"end":173}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/drive.rs","range":{"start":267,"end":311}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:6819f07e7d61fd0bea8df7a32b6ad1c35bf71b6dc98c85c989f0e7d95b48460c","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":77449,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788596824257818-0003279353-000000/PRD-83","revision":"sha256:bb6c3b4d15d3f6b3aaf9e7769d13ddd95f44c636916f1a88f765e85dfa5f687d","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-6819f07e7d61fd0bea8df7a32b6ad1c35bf71b6dc98c85c989f0e7d95b48460c-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Collect every pending row whose `prd_id` matches the selection and either apply the decision to all of them under one actor/reason, or reject the ambiguous selection with a message naming the ordinals involved. Either way, after a decision, re-report how many findings for that PRD remain pending. Add a regression seeding two findings under one `prd_id` and asserting that a PRD-id selection does not leave a silently undecided row.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"F3-attended-pause-no-fallback","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Attended drive pause prints no approve/reject command when the interactive decision aborts or fails","claim":"In `handle_scope_pause`, the `attended` branch calls `list_or_decide_interactively` and then returns unconditionally — on success, on a blank \"preserve\" answer, and on error. The unattended branch is the only path that prints the paste-runnable commands. `list_or_decide_interactively` returns `Err` for an actor missing the `human:` prefix, an empty reason, or an unmatched selection, and returns `Ok(())` for a blank selection; in all of those cases the attended operator is left with a one-line failure message (or nothing at all) and no hash-bound command they can retry with, which is precisely the dead end acceptance criterion 3 and 5 exist to close. A mistyped actor at a terminal is a likelier outcome than an unattended run, and it is the one case with no remedy on screen.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/drive.rs","range":{"start":267,"end":311}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/cli/scope_decisions.rs","range":{"start":67,"end":173}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:6819f07e7d61fd0bea8df7a32b6ad1c35bf71b6dc98c85c989f0e7d95b48460c","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":77449,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788596824257818-0003279353-000000/PRD-83","revision":"sha256:bb6c3b4d15d3f6b3aaf9e7769d13ddd95f44c636916f1a88f765e85dfa5f687d","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-6819f07e7d61fd0bea8df7a32b6ad1c35bf71b6dc98c85c989f0e7d95b48460c-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Have the attended branch fall through to the `scope_decision_commands` printing whenever the interactive surface returns an error or leaves findings undecided, so every scope pause ends with either a recorded decision or a runnable command. Pin it with a regression that drives `handle_scope_pause(attended = true)` with an invalid actor and asserts the approve and reject commands appear in the output.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"F4-unrelated-daemon-signal-change","category":"scope_violation","severity":"low","blocking":false,"title":"Unrelated termination-signal registration change in main.rs","claim":"`run_with_tray_feature_but_disabled` is rewritten to construct `TerminationSignals::register().expect(\"register termination signals\")` and pass it into `daemon_run`. Nothing in the objective or the five acceptance criteria concerns daemon lifecycle or signal handling, no other changed file touches `daemon_run`'s signature, and no test in `tests/scope_approval_ux.rs` exercises this path. The change is inside `allowed_paths` (`crates/`) but is a drive-by edit to an unrelated subsystem, and it introduces a panicking `expect` on a startup path that the required test check explicitly skips (`--skip daemon_starts_and_stops_on_sigterm`), so the candidate carries an unreviewed behavioral change with no accompanying coverage.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/main.rs","range":{"start":637,"end":645}}],"remediation":"Split this hunk out of the PRD-083 candidate. If it is a genuine fix for a broken tray-feature build path, land it as its own change with a test that exercises the signal path; if it is incidental, revert it here.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"F5-gate-json-field-rewrite","category":"unverifiable_claim","severity":"low","blocking":false,"title":"Human-gate items are re-serialized field-by-field, which may drop fields from the gate struct","claim":"`list_pending_human_gates` previously placed the values returned by `pending_human_gates` into `items` directly, serialized by their own `Serialize` implementation. The new code consumes each gate and rebuilds a `json!` object naming exactly six fields (`kind`, `session_id`, `prd_id`, `prd_path`, `detail`, `recovery_commands`). Whether that is lossless depends on the field list of the gate type, which is defined in `familiar-ai-storage` and is not part of this package, so I cannot confirm it. If the struct carries any additional field (a checkpoint id, timestamp, or attempt sequence), the JSON contract of `familiar-ai stewardship gates` silently loses it, and the new tests only assert `recovery_commands`, `prd_id`, and the presence of `scope-decisions`, so nothing would catch the loss.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/stewardship.rs","range":{"start":388,"end":415}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:6819f07e7d61fd0bea8df7a32b6ad1c35bf71b6dc98c85c989f0e7d95b48460c","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":77449,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788596824257818-0003279353-000000/PRD-83","revision":"sha256:bb6c3b4d15d3f6b3aaf9e7769d13ddd95f44c636916f1a88f765e85dfa5f687d","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-6819f07e7d61fd0bea8df7a32b6ad1c35bf71b6dc98c85c989f0e7d95b48460c-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Prefer mutating the gate's own `recovery_commands` and serializing the struct (`serde_json::to_value(gate)`) so the JSON contract stays derived from one definition; if the manual `json!` is kept, add a regression asserting the full key set of a gate item so a future field addition fails loudly rather than silently disappearing from the CLI output.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"F6-advisory-suite-failing","category":"unverifiable_claim","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Workspace test suite fails at the candidate with no baseline to attribute it","claim":"`tests-workspace-advisory` (`cargo test --workspace --no-default-features --no-fail-fast`) exits 101 at the candidate revision. The required `tests-green-crates` check passes only because it restricts to five crates and skips `daemon_starts_and_stops_on_sigterm` — the very test covering the daemon signal path this candidate modifies in `main.rs`. The package contains no base-revision run of the advisory check, so I cannot tell from the disclosed evidence whether the failure is pre-existing or introduced here, and the one unrelated hunk in this candidate lands squarely in the area the required check excludes. That coincidence is worth resolving before disposition rather than after.","evidence":[{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-workspace-advisory","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:ce1344a8e4be1628d767b03dabe3e78ca252ba4e7dc85ed27b07f6b790bb337c","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":113499,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788596824257818-0003279353-000000/PRD-83","revision":"sha256:bb6c3b4d15d3f6b3aaf9e7769d13ddd95f44c636916f1a88f765e85dfa5f687d","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-ce1344a8e4be1628d767b03dabe3e78ca252ba4e7dc85ed27b07f6b790bb337c-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/main.rs","range":{"start":637,"end":645}}],"remediation":"Run the advisory workspace suite at base revision dfa1b220d60a8a28047a26f6fee80b7dec8cc0cf and attach both runs, or name the failing tests and state explicitly that they fail identically at base. If any failure is new, fix it or drop the unrelated `main.rs` hunk and re-run.","status":"open","supersedes":null}]}
